## t/test_gui.stk — assertion tests for the stryke `GUI` package.
##
## Uses stryke's native test framework (`assert_*` + `test_run`).
## Exercises the FFI surface without needing input/screen permissions:
## `keyboard_keys` is a pure dispatch into a static key-name table inside
## the cdylib (no enigo init, no platform input handle), so it runs
## unattended in CI.
##
## The mouse/keyboard/pixel/screenshot ops themselves need OS permission
## (macOS Accessibility + Screen Recording, Wayland portals), so they are
## not asserted here — they can't run unattended in CI.

use GUI

# ── GUI::keyboard_keys FFI roundtrip (permission-free) ─────────────
# Proves the FFI call path end-to-end: stryke → gui__key_keys → JSON → stryke.
val @keys = GUI::keyboard_keys()
assert_gt scalar(@keys), 100, "GUI::keyboard_keys returns >100 names"

# ── well-known key names are present ───────────────────────────────
val %seen = map { $_ => 1 } @keys
assert_ok $seen{enter}, "keys contain 'enter'"
assert_ok $seen{shift}, "keys contain 'shift'"
assert_ok $seen{f1},    "keys contain 'f1'"

# ── function keys f1..f24 ──────────────────────────────────────────
val @fkeys = grep { /^f\d+$/ } @keys
assert_ge scalar(@fkeys), 24, "f1..f24 all present"

test_run
